{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786504427,"nonce":"UpqsOzZaj7jEHEkqyh/Lbzd1NaU=","target":"7b57c2200cbbfca10f5edb05725f601ec2389709c33fdc0aedd4711cffd20ec3","message":"**What is wrong.** The fūrin is meant to swing once when oto starts listening — a single damped rotation on the header mark, still at rest thereafter. This ticket is **blocked**, not merely unimplemented, and it is blocked twice: the intended trigger does not mean what it reads as, and the SPEC does not currently permit a second animation to exist. Both blockers are recorded here so that whoever picks this up does not rediscover them, and so that nobody ships the obvious version.\n\n**The demonstration.**\n\n*Blocker 1 — cleared.* The two glyph copies were optical compensation, not sloppiness, and a naive shared `viewBox` halved the header stroke: 1.6 × (16/40) = 0.64 px against today's 1.20 px. This is now resolved. `web/src/components/ui/Chime.tsx:61-64` carries per-size stroke constants (3 for `mark`, 1.6 for `glyph`) with the arithmetic recorded at `:9-28`, and `Chime.test.tsx:52-86` pins both. Motion work no longer has to solve the glyph. Treat this blocker as discharged.\n\n*Blocker 2 — sub-pixel detail, still live.* The mark renders a 40-unit box at 16 px, scale 0.4. Anything added for the swing to act on is below one device pixel at 1× DPR: a tanzaku paper strip at 5×11 units becomes 2.0 × 4.4 px with a 0.48 px stroke, and at the proposed opacity 0.55 that is roughly 26% effective coverage — a grey smudge, not a strip. A clapper ball at r=1.1 becomes 0.88 px across. A portrait 24×40 box would make the mark's content 9.6 px wide beside the 15 px bold wordmark at `AppShell.tsx:303`, about 40% smaller than today. `Chime.tsx:75-79` already records this and restricts the zetsu to `glyph`. So the thing that swings is a two-stroke dome and mouth arc: whatever motion is chosen has to be legible on that alone.\n\n*Blocker 3 — the real one. `connecting → live` is not a page-load signal.* `web/src/api/stream.ts:309` reads `this.#setState(this.#lastSeq === null ? \"connecting\" : \"reconnecting\")`, and `#lastSeq` is seeded in the constructor from `sessionStorage` — `stream.ts:200` calls `readResumePoint()`, defined at `stream.ts:429`. `connecting` therefore means \"this tab has no resume point\", not \"this page just loaded\". Both directions fail:\n\n- A reload of a tab that ever received a frame has a resume point, so it goes `idle → reconnecting → live` and the swing **never fires** — precisely for the returning operator, the person the mark is for. A cold start whose first fetch fails loses it too: `#scheduleRetry` runs on `!res.ok` and the state leaves `connecting` before the stream is ever live.\n- On a quiet install `#lastSeq` stays `null` indefinitely, because it is only ever set on a sequenced frame (`stream.ts:377`) and heartbeats do not carry one. Every reconnect then re-enters `connecting`, and reconnects are frequent: `online` (`stream.ts:274`), tab visibility becoming visible while not live (`stream.ts:287`), the 50 s stall check running every 10 s (`stream.ts:290-296`, `STALL_TIMEOUT_MS` at `:85`), and a clean end-of-stream (`stream.ts:353-354`), plus every deploy. The bell would swing on tab focus, all day, on the install with the least going on.\n\nThere is a second objection to this trigger independent of its reliability: motion on a connection transition is *state* motion, not brand motion. `AppShell.tsx` keeps connection health in Tier A deliberately, and a silent visual channel for \"we went live\" beside the labelled `aria-live` badge cuts against U1 (`SPEC.md:4862`), which requires at least two *labelled* channels.\n\n*Blocker 4 — governance.* SPEC §N (`docs/design/SPEC.md:5083`) binds an ADR, the SPEC edit and any `CONTEXT.md` update into the same commit, and closes at `:5098` with: *\"Implementers who find an ambiguity MUST raise it rather than choose. An undocumented choice made by one agent is a compile error or a silent behavioural divergence for the next four.\"* This is that ambiguity. U4 (`SPEC.md:4865`) legislates only urgency motion and names exactly one animation; acceptance criterion 47 (`SPEC.md:3875-3876`) says *\"the only urgency motion is the unacked-critical pulse\"*; the §M.7 motion gate (`SPEC.md:5080`) asserts *\"the unacked-critical pulse is absent when the media query is set\"* and knows of no other. Non-urgency motion is unlegislated rather than permitted — and four instances already ship unlegislated (`AppShell.tsx:56`, `primitives.tsx:87`, `states.tsx:62`, `Dialog.tsx:109`).\n\n**Why it matters.** The decision on how to legislate has been taken and should not be relitigated: **add a new U9; do not amend U4.** U4's absolute \"no flashing, no blinking, ever\" stays unqualified, because the missing rule is about a whole class of motion, not an exception to that sentence. The counter-argument is recorded and is the reason for the ordering: a U9 written to bless a wind-bell is a rule written to fit a feature. So the honest sequence is a motion ADR first, with U9 as its consequence.\n\nDraft U9, to be written by the ADR rather than lifted from this ticket: *non-urgency motion is permitted only if it is one-shot, at most 200 ms, confined to opacity and transform, free of luminance oscillation, absent under `prefers-reduced-motion`, and never the sole carrier of a state fact.* Note what that draft costs: the researched 1400 ms damped swing does not qualify, and neither does the connection trigger — the last clause rules it out on its own. The ADR either accepts a much shorter bell or argues its way past its own rule, and it should also account for the four instances already shipping.\n\nTwo smaller facts for whoever writes it. Rotating an inner `\u003cg\u003e` clips, because the SVG viewport is `overflow: hidden`, and `overflow: visible` collides with the wordmark 6 px away. And a reduced-motion line naming `.oto-chime-swing` in the block at `index.css:141-142` would be a no-op: `index.css:145-152` already forces `animation-duration: .01ms !important` and `animation-iteration-count: 1 !important` on `*`, so any new animation is already neutralised without being named.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/api/stream.ts:200`, `:274`, `:287`, `:290-296`, `:309`, `:353-354`, `:429`; `web/src/components/AppShell.tsx:302-303`; `web/src/components/ui/Chime.tsx:61-64`, `:75-79`; `web/src/index.css:109-153`; `docs/design/SPEC.md:3875-3876`, `:4862`, `:4865`, `:5080`, `:5083-5098`.\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** A motion ADR in `docs/adr/` states the rule for non-urgency motion and accounts for the four instances already shipping; U9 lands in `docs/design/SPEC.md` as that ADR's consequence, in the same commit, with U4 untouched, and AC-47 and the §M.7 gate updated so they no longer imply exactly one animation; the swing's trigger is a genuine once-per-session event owned by the component — mount, not a `stream.ts` connection transition — such that neither a reload with a resume point nor a tab focus on a quiet install can change whether it fires; and a test asserts it fires exactly once per session and not at all under reduced motion.","files":null}]}